From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 10:55:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: reading a directory is an invalid device request

NtReadFile left a directory handle to the generic read path, which relies on read() failing with
EISDIR, as Linux's does, to return STATUS_INVALID_DEVICE_REQUEST. FreeBSD's read() on a directory
can succeed with the directory's raw entries, and on the PS5 ntdll's file test saw NtReadFile on a
directory return STATUS_SUCCESS (file.c:197). The server already tells a directory apart
(FD_TYPE_DIR), so the status is now returned before any read, on every system.
---
  dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c | 8 ++++++++
  1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c
index 74f270d..768c6bb 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/file.c
@@ -6593,6 +6593,14 @@ NTSTATUS WINAPI NtReadFile( HANDLE handle, HANDLE event, PIO_APC_ROUTINE apc, vo
 
     async_read = !(options & (FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_ALERT | FILE_SYNCHRONOUS_IO_NONALERT));
 
+    /* Reading a directory is not a request a directory takes. Linux's read() says so
+     * (EISDIR); FreeBSD's, the PS5's, can return the directory's raw entries instead. */
+    if (type == FD_TYPE_DIR)
+    {
+        status = STATUS_INVALID_DEVICE_REQUEST;
+        goto done;
+    }
+
     if (type == FD_TYPE_FILE)
     {
         if (async_read && (!offset || offset->QuadPart < 0))
